.TH QUECTO 1 "March 2026" "quecto 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
quecto \- a small terminal text editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B quecto
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-h ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B quecto
is a small text editor for the terminal. It opens
.I file
for editing, or starts an empty buffer if the name is omitted or the file
does not exist yet. The whole editor is a few hundred lines of C with no
dependencies beyond the standard library, which makes it easy to read, easy
to audit, and small enough to fit on a rescue image.
.PP
Text is edited in place on the screen: there are no modes, no menus and no
prompts except when a file needs a name. The cursor follows the text, the
display soft-wraps long lines, and the status bar at the bottom of the
screen shows either the file name and cursor position or a short message.
.PP
Text is handled as UTF-8 throughout. Characters outside the ASCII range
occupy the number of columns they are drawn in: a Chinese or Japanese
character takes two columns, a combining accent takes none.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Print the version number and exit.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Ctrl+W
Save the buffer. If it has no name yet, ask for one first.
.TP
.B Ctrl+N
Save under a different name and continue editing that file.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Q
Quit. If there are unsaved changes, the first press warns and the second
one discards them.
.TP
.B Ctrl+K
Insert a character by its Unicode code point. Accepts decimal
.RB ( 20013 )
or hexadecimal with a
.B 0x
prefix
.RB ( 0x4e2d ).
.TP
.B Ctrl+V
Show the version in the status bar.
.TP
.B Ctrl+L
Redraw the screen. Every key already redraws it, so this is only useful
after the terminal has been upset by something else.
.TP
.B Arrow keys
Move the cursor, one character at a time. Moving up or down keeps the
column, so passing through a short line does not move the cursor left for
good.
.TP
.B Home, End
Jump to the start or the end of the line.
.TP
.B PageUp, PageDown
Move up or down by one screenful.
.TP
.B Backspace
Delete the character before the cursor. At the start of a line, join it
with the line above.
.TP
.B Delete
Delete the character under the cursor. At the end of a line, join it with
the line below.
.TP
.B Tab
Insert a tab character.
.TP
.B Esc
Ignored.
.SH TEXT HANDLING
.SS UTF-8
A character is treated as one character, whatever its length in bytes.
The cursor steps over a Chinese character in one move, one press of
Backspace removes the whole character, and a character is never split
across two lines.
.PP
The locale is not consulted: the editor always reads and writes UTF-8, and
always asks the terminal to draw UTF-8. This is what makes it behave the
same over ssh, in a container and on a console.
.PP
Files that are not valid UTF-8 still open. Bytes that do not form a
character are shown as a single replacement glyph and are written back
unchanged, so the editor never quietly destroys data it does not
understand.
.SS Tabs
Tabs are drawn as spaces up to the next tab stop, which is every
.B 3
columns, counted from the left edge of the text. The file keeps its tab
characters; only the display is expanded. This is deliberate: a tab is a
jump to a column, not a printable symbol, and showing it as
.B ^I
would misrepresent the layout of the file.
.SS Line endings
When a file is read, CRLF and lone CR line endings are treated as line
breaks. When it is written, every line ends with a single LF, and the file
ends with a newline.
.SH STATUS BAR
The bottom line shows the file name, a
.B *
if there are unsaved changes, and the cursor position as
.IR line , column .
The column counts display columns, so a tab counts as the number of spaces
it occupies and a wide character counts as two.
.PP
When there is something to report \(em a save, an error, a version number
\(em the message replaces the file name until the next key is pressed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/quecto
The installed binary. The manual shows the default prefix; packaging
systems are expected to use their own.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/q
Symlink to the same binary, for people who like short commands.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit after Ctrl+Q, or after \-\-help or \-\-version.
.TP
.B 1
The terminal could not be configured, a write failed, or the arguments
were not understood.
.SH EXAMPLES
Open a file, or create it if it does not exist:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
quecto notes.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Start with an empty buffer and name it when saving for the first time:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
q
.EE
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
No syntax highlighting, no undo, no search, no multiple buffers. These are
deliberate choices rather than omissions: the point of the program is to
be small enough to read in one sitting.
.PP
The editor keeps the file in memory and rewrites it from scratch on save.
Saving is not atomic: if the program is killed while writing, the file is
left truncated.
.PP
A terminal is assumed to understand standard ANSI escapes and to measure
text the same way this editor does. Terminals that draw emoji or ambiguous
width characters differently will show the cursor out of step on those
lines only.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vi (1),
.BR nano (1),
.BR ed (1)
